<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Votadini</h1><p class="st-meta">80 CE – 450 CE (approx.) · capital Traprain Law</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">People of south-east Scotland and Northumberland centred on Traprain Law, who lived beside Roman Britain and became the post-Roman Gododdin.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ptolemy places the Votadini on the east coast between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-forth">Firth of Forth</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tyne">Tyne</a> in the 2nd century. They descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> communities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothian">Lothian</a> and the Borders, and their great hillfort at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/traprain-law">Traprain Law</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-lothian">East Lothian</a> was a major centre from before the Roman arrival in the 80s.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Unlike neighbours such as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/selgovae">Selgovae</a>, the Votadini show few signs of hostility to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and many historians think they were a friendly buffer people beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall</a>. Roman goods reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/traprain-law">Traprain Law</a> in quantity, culminating in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/traprain-law-treasure">Traprain Law Treasure</a>, a hoard of cut Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> buried in the early 5th century, perhaps a payment or subsidy. Their economy rested on mixed farming.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After Roman rule ended, the Votadini developed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-gododdin">Kingdom of Gododdin</a>, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/din-eidyn">Din Eidyn</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>, whose warriors are celebrated in the Welsh poem Y Gododdin. Their name in its Welsh form, Guotodin, carried the people into the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a>.</p></div><aside 